<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Brandenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">948 CE – 1571 CE (approx.) · capital Ziesar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ottonian missionary bishopric on the Havel that, after a long exile, became a small church territory absorbed by the Hohenzollern electors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Otto I founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 948 at the captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-the-hevelli">Hevelli</a> stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brenna</a>, as part of his eastern mission, placing it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in 968. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-slav-rising">Great Slav Rising</a> of 983 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> destroyed the see, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> lived as titular exiles until the 12th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1157, the Premonstratensian cathedral chapter was re-established, and the cathedral on the island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a> was begun in 1165. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> held a small territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziesar">Ziesar</a>, where they built their residence, but the margraves dominated them and claimed the right to nominate bishops.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> became Lutheran under Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector">Joachim II</a> from 1539, and the last Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-von-jagow">Matthias von Jagow</a>, accepted the change. The prince-bishopric was secularised and fully incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg</a> by 1571. The cathedral chapter continued as a Protestant institution.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Brandenburg" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Gregor Rom · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_des_Hochstifts_Brandenburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>948 CE – 1571 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ziesar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Matthias von Jagow</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Brandenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-brandenburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Brandenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4962 369 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Brandenburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5282 625L5229 694M5282 625L5282 625"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5282 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-brandenburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
